ial events planning  and we use them to block roadways on a  regular basis and it's it's quite  frankly rather than two police cars uh  we can use a bus and so that's why  they're there to obstruct or not to  obstruct but to impede and block traffic  for safety purposes  thank you  and the follow-up question to you as  well uh chief framer there have been a  few a few posts on social media but i'm  thinking of one particularly by mpp  randy hillier showing gas cans and  explosive shells in reference to the  convoy tomorrow are these posts being  considered as any type of terrorism type  threat  um  what i will say and it's a good question  thank you what i will say is that we are  engaged with all our partners across the  province examining social media all in  intelligence that is available and based  on the information we have we will  respond  and to ensure public safety  thank you and we'll go to our last  reporter now uh jackie jackie crandall's  from cp24 do you want to ask your  question  i think an apologies if any of these  questions are redundant i haven't been  able to to listen in much i have a  question about how long you'll let the  um the protesters stay you've seen  obviously in ottawa that they're  sticking around um you know the issue of  vehicles aside if they're able to get in  on foot and you know large numbers of  them can do that how do you control that  type of crowd and how long do you let  them stay if their intention is to stay  uh okay thanks uh jackie for that i uh  we'll we'll assess the event as it goes  i mean if people are on foot and they're  demonstrating around queen's park  usually these will go on for a number of  hours and and then  we'll be looking to  see the people move on and and we'll  work with the crowds and the organizers  and trying to facilitate a peaceful  protest but our intention is that  these areas will not be used for people  to encamp or  stay permanently for any duration of  time  and do you have a follow-up j